<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Temple of the Obelisks</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">early 2nd millennium BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Temple of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">Obelisks</a> is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-bronze-age">Middle Bronze Age</a> sanctuary at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, named after the many standing stones erected in its courtyard. Hundreds of offerings were found buried there, including bronze figurines of gods, some covered in gold leaf, weapons and jewellery. The archaeologist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurice-dunand">Maurice Dunand</a> moved the temple to a new site to excavate the older temple beneath it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/temple-of-the-obelisks.jpg" alt="Temple of the Obelisks" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jerzy Strzelecki · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Byblos(js)_6.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The temple stands on the remains of an older sanctuary that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurice-dunand">Maurice Dunand</a> called the L-shaped Temple, after the arrangement of its two rooms and courtyard. That building was put up around 2600 BCE, about two centuries after the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baalat-gebal">Baalat Gebal</a> some 100 metres to the west, and had well-built walls. It was dedicated to the male tutelary god of the city and is thought to have burned down at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-bronze-age">Early Bronze Age</a>. The later temple kept its general outline but was built with irregular walls, and it remained in use from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-bronze-age">Middle Bronze Age</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> or later.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>It was an open-air sanctuary, its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">obelisks</a> and standing stones set in a court around the cella. Unlike its predecessor it had no single god to worship and probably followed the tradition of the Syro-Palestinian cult. It was one of seven main cult buildings around the central spring and artificial lake of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a>, each perhaps the heart of a neighbourhood. While the neighbouring Temple of Lady Byblos yielded many Egyptian goods, this temple and the L-shaped Temple show designs inspired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, which suggests that parts of the city dealt with different regions. A jeweller's workshop and the mass of finds point to trade or other economic activity there.</p><p>Its best-known monument is the Abishemu Obelisk, a limestone obelisk of around 1800 BCE commemorating King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abishemu-i">Abishemu I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a>. Its Egyptian hieroglyphic inscription is read as a dedication to Heryshaf, widely though not universally taken here as the Egyptian form of the Canaanite war god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/resheph">Resheph</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Dunand identified the temple and excavated it over several decades from 1924. Most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">obelisks</a> still stood upright in their original places, and a few lay buried in a favissa, a pit for votive deposits. To reach the L-shaped Temple he dismantled the upper building and set it up again 40 metres to the east. Almost all the finds are in the National Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>. The poor excavation record and Dunand's unfinished publications have made later study of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> harder.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Temple of the Obelisks” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Middle Bronze Age the city’s craftsmen produced fine jewellery, weapons and bronze figurines of gods, hundreds of which, some covered in gold leaf, were buried as offerings in the <b>Temple of the Obelisks</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
